gh  performance computing that you have  available through  ultrascan you can address these  multi-wavelength data challenges that we  have  and one direction multi-wavelength  applications  include protein nucleic acid  interactions proteins with fluorescent  protein fusions like for example gfp  or rfp or yfp and you can also use  fluorescent tags if there's enough  tag on there you get enough of an  extinction that you can follow that as  well  or you can use proteins which have  intrinsic chromophores  uh maybe in the visible range where they  wouldn't overlap with the uv  spectrum for example a heme does that  there could be others mixtures  containing small interacting molecules  like drugs for example  that may have a very strong absorbance  in the uv and that may be different from  protein absorbance so that could be  followed as well  you can study nanoparticle loading viral  vector loading  and the separation of spectrally  distinct solutes in mixtures  for composition identification  so um i'm at the end of my talk and i  just  would like to put in a quick plug for  the 25th international auc conference  which will take place july 11 through  16th  next year next summer here in lethbridge  um we will be hosting at uh please mark  your calendar and  follow this website right here auc  2021.uleth.ca  for any updates and i want to thank you  for your attention if you have any  questions that we can't answer here  today  feel free to send them to my personal  email dml gmail.com  and i would also like to credit some of  my collaborators  um in my group emory brooks gary gorbad  kip j and alexis savalia have  done the programming on the ultrascan  software  my daughter aisha was involved in the  analysis  of the bsa and dna mixtures  amy runs our lab here and help with all  the other experiments that you saw if  she didn't do them herself  as does christiana and marielle  carmen on is uh  was postdoc in my lab and worked on the  cytochrome c  sample on also together with sandy ross  and bruce bola from the university of  montana  wyoming chow at the university of texas  in san antonio  um helped us with the finite element  solutions and  inward curve and joe pearson ing and car  gudak  developed the multi-wavelengths detector  of the first generation the curfew  optics um that can acquire all the data  all at once  uh at georgia state university the group  of uh marcus garman and michael brink  uh brenton were involved with the west  nile virus study  and the nr um  protein dna interaction was done by  chris horn and renwick adoptions lab at  the university of canterbury  and at the university of british  columbia we're working together with  peter collis angelico connie  and dominic ritzigman to  study the lipid nanoparticles and i want  to also thank eric from segrent from  beckmann culture engineering  who helped us with the data acquisition  uh  integration of ultrascan on the optima  we have funding from the national  science foundation the nih  uh tera grid exceed for the high  performance computing allocations  um canada foundation for innovation  uh the national science and engineering  research council in canada  alberta innovates and the canadian  institute for health research so we want  to appreciate all  we i want to thank all of those and  appreciate their support  and finally i'd like to show a  disclaimer page that pikmin asked me to  show  and thank you again for your interest  and  to see you again sometime  thank you dr tembler for your  informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen we will answer as  many of your questions as we have time  for all right so let's get started our  first question  is how many wavelengths can you acquire  with the optima auc and what limitations  exist  so uh the number of wavelengths that you  can acquire  depends on which software you use  if you use the beckman software on you  can collect 20 wavelengths  per channel with the  ultrascan software you can go up to 100  however there are limitations and the  limitations are primarily dictated by  the  by the scanning speed with which you can  collect the data  since all wavelengths are collected  sequentially you need to allow for  time to collect all of the wavelengths  that you want to  measure and the more wavelengths you  collect the longer it will take  per scan so if you  were to measure 100 wavelengths it would  really take a long time  to get to the second scan because you  have to scan  all the 100 wavelengths for for the  first scan  and then the second scan and so forth so  the number of scans that you will get  for each individual wavelengths will  decrease and there's another limitation  which is that for each wavelength that  you want to collect  on the instrument has to do  a calibration at the beginning of the  run  which takes about a minute per  wavelength so you can  maybe not that long but it'll take a  certain amount of time which will delay  the start of the scanning  so um it is it depends on the  experimental conditions how slow you can  run  and how much resolution you need from  your experiment  in order to decide how many wavelengths  are practical to be collected on the  optima  thank you okay let's go on to the next  question dr tumblr  uh here goes i'm studying a protein plus  small molecule system  i have recorded one experiment with  absorbance data acquired at 280  nanometers  and another experiment with absorbance  data acquired at 490 nanometers  which is my small molecule absorption  maximum peak  now can i acquire these two independent  experiments  uh sorry can i combine these two  independent experiments  for multi-wavelength analysis  yes in principle that's perfectly  adequate to do that  the question is whether it's sufficient  to  get a separation and that depends on the  spectrum  of the protein or the molecule and  and the other interacting component the  small molecule  and if they don't have any overlaps or  if the um  resolution or i should say the  separation of the spectra is sufficient  as described in my talk  um then this will work otherwise you  will have to add additional wavelengths  to make the  separation more distinct so it really  depends on the angle  between the two two spectra that you  have so  it should be as large as possible  so an extension of that question is the  next one  is there any way that i can incorporate  the interference data which i've  acquired at the same time as a 260  as well as a 280 nanometer absorbance  experiment as in can i use the intensity  data set  as a pseudo wavelength data set and then  incorporate it into the multi-wavelength  analysis  no not really um the problem with that  is is that  you don't really know what the  refractive index is of every species in  your mixture  and what you see is essentially a  collection of all of the  refractive indices um in the  in the mixture at every point in the in  the cell  and since there is no way to  optically deconvolute them and assign  like let's say molar concentrations to  them  um this is an unreliable way of adding  information  and it's it's really not a good way to  to perform your multi-wavelength  experiment  okay so as an extension of that question  dr daimler again  uh since each component has to be  fingerprinted  via a uv-vis spectrophotometer in order  to do really good multi-wavelength  analysis  supposing we had a way to specifically  say that we have  components x y and z in our  sample which have the refractive indexes  of r1  r2 and r3 uh which are recorded  independently not  in the auc instrument would that help  um it's it's not really  the way the software is designed to be  able to uh  incorporate that data that is not what  we're trying to do here  we're just uh looking at the observance  specter for which we have  precise molar on molar descriptions  for each of the species  at every wavelength that is being  measured if we had that for the  for the refractive indices of all of the  species  um maybe there is a way to include that  but  the software is not designed to handle  that  all right there's one more question we  have time for and it  is this is there a trade-off between  spectral angle  and the number of wavelengths to give a  good result  well in general the larger the angle the  better  and it just depends on how much  resolution  and how much separation you really  expect to uh  to have and you need in order to  conclusively decide what  species the spectrum should be assigned  to or the concentration should be  assigned to  so the more species you include uh the  more wave  the more species with different spectra  you're trying to  deconvolute from your from your system  the more  separation you should have if you just  have two species like protein and dna  you can go to fairly low angles and  still get satisfactory results  so in general what you want to do is  look for those regions in the spectrum  where you get the most difference and  then pick those wavelengths  and include those and then use that  angle calculator and ultra scan to  figure out  how much difference you can get from  different combinations of wavelengths  and then just simply pick the one with  the smallest number and the  smallest number of wavelengths and the  largest uh degree of separation and  those will be the best ones to use  thank you unfortunately it seems that we  have  run out of time so i want to remind our  audience  that the questions we were unable to  answer today and those that will be  coming in to us during the on-demand  period that the presentation will be  available on lab roots  will all be answered by dr tumbler and  sent to you  at the email address you provided at the  time of registration  dr dimbler do you have any final  comments for our audience  well i'd just like to say that we feel  that multi-wavelength is a  an exciting new toolbox for auc and  uh that there will likely be many more  uh  important applications that can be  solved with it and  so i'd like you to think about how you  might be able to incorporate it into  your own uh  research as well and if you have any  questions feel free to contact me  thank you mr tumbler so before we before  we wrap up i'd like to thank the  audience today for joining us  and for their amazing questions uh again  we will try to get to all of the  questions that you've submitted  via the question box and answer you  directly  to the email address that you used at  the time of registration and we want to  thank dr daimler again  for his time today and for his many many  contributions to auc research and we'd  also like to extend our thanks to labruz  as well as our sponsor beckman cultural  life sciences for underwriting today's  educational webcast  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  roots will alert you via email when it's  available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event thank you for joining  us again  goodbye have a nice day
 

